fact WorkItem(id: "WI-923", description: some(value: "`anthill query --mode domain _global` names the top-level domain by a KERNEL TAG the name ladder can never return, so the CLI matches that one spelling by hand (anthill-cli/src/main.rs, the `--mode domain` arm, WI-914). Measured: 139 of 140 distinct `by_domain` keys in a stdlib load are DECLARED symbols (a namespace or a sort), which is why WI-914 could put that mode on `resolve_name_in_global`; the 140th is `_global`, a raw `intern(\"_global\")` from `make_name_term` that `define` never registers, holding 242 clauses on the stdlib alone. The hand-match keeps those listable without re-admitting a silent fallback for every unresolvable name, but it is a SECOND SPELLING of a scope identity the loader already owns, and WI-908's lesson is that a separately-spelled rung is how a mount comes to take a functor its author never named. NOT DRIVEN. FIRST STEP: check whether `_global` can simply BE a declared symbol (a `define_qualified_only` Namespace at bootstrap, beside KERNEL_META_SORTS) — if it can, the ladder answers it and the CLI arm deletes; if it cannot, find out what that would make resolvable (`_global.foo` as a path) and whether the loader's scope-term identity survives it. ACCEPTANCE: either the CLI's `_global` arm is gone because the ladder answers, or the reason it cannot is stated where the arm is; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-923", author: "user", content: "WI-921 SHARPENED THIS, and left a false claim of mine as the reason to. Deleting `--mode sort` removed the one MODE whose argument was not a name, and I wrote that up in six places as 'the last position in `query` where `-i` is inert'. That is FALSE, and the counterexample is this ticket's own subject: `--mode domain _global` (main.rs, the Domain arm) still takes `kb.intern(name)` by hand instead of `resolve_listing_name`, so `-i` cannot bear on that one spelling. Caught by review, corrected at all six sites (the enum doc, `resolve_listing_name`'s doc, kernel-language.md 8.6, and three test files), each now naming `_global` as the surviving exception and pointing here.\n\nWHAT CHANGED FOR WI-923: the asymmetry is now the whole of it, and it is WORSE than the deleted mode's. `--mode sort` was inert for `-i` and REFUSED it loudly (WI-853's rule). `--mode domain _global` is inert for `-i` and SILENTLY ACCEPTS it. So the one instance of the inert-flag rule that survives in `query` is the one instance that does not obey it — and WI-921 deleted the test that pinned the rule (`wi853_query_import_test::an_import_flag_is_refused_where_it_cannot_apply`) because its subject was the mode, recording exactly this at the deletion site. Either `_global` becomes declared (this ticket's proposal, after which the arm and the exception both delete) or the arm must refuse `-i` the way the deleted mode did; doing neither leaves the rule with a live counterexample and no pin.", at: "2026-08-01T20:52:07Z")
